    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d558">

                <pb facs="0609" n="599" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg599"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/8–844: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the
                        Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-08T21:00:00-04:00">August 8, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1886. British Embassy here states that instructions have been sent by Foreign
                    Office, London to Clark Kerr to join with you in inviting the Soviet Government
                    to go as far as it feels able in supporting the Anglo-American <hi
                        rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> in Stockholm referred to in Department’s
                    1822, July 31.<anchor n="16" xml:id="d558-f1"/> You are authorized to join with
                    your British colleague in making this approach to the Soviet Government as soon
                    as possible.</p>
                <p>Sent to Moscow, repeated to London and to Stockholm.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d558-f1" n="16" xml:id="d558-f1-content">
                <p>Same as telegram 6018 to London, <ref target="#pg590">p. 590</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
